3️⃣ yield() Method
🔹 What is yield()?
yield() tells the CPU:
“I am willing to pause and let other threads run.”
🔹 Why do we use yield()?
To improve fairnes
To avoid one thread hogging the CPU
Mostly used in CPU-intensive tasks

📌 Important:
yield() is just a request, CPU may ignore it.
🔹
 Real-Life Example

Classroom example:

One student is speaking continuously

Teacher says: “Let others speak”

That’s yield()

🔹 Who controls which thread runs?
✅ Operating System Scheduler

Important interview line:

Java only creates threads; the OS decides when and how they run.
yield() does NOT guarantee order
It only increases the chance of switching
